libstdoc news
===============

unreleased
----------

Changes made after the 1.0.0-beta release.  Nothing here is released
yet.


Bug fixes
~~~~~~~~~

  * io/syscall_linux.c: a system call that completed a transfer of 4096
    bytes or more was reported as a failure.  The raw kernel result was
    classified by comparing it, as an unsigned integer, against 4096 -
    the magnitude of the smallest kernel error rather than the error
    itself - so every count above that became "-1" and the bytes were
    silently treated as lost.  Nothing in the library wrote more than
    256 bytes at a time (the stream staging buffer is 256 bytes), so the
    defect was invisible from the public interface and reachable only
    through stdoc_sys_write.  The comparison is now against -4095, which
    puts every failure in the top 4095 values of the unsigned range and
    leaves every count below it.  test/largewrite.c pins the boundary.
  * test/heap.c: the printf-scratch scenario used a format string whose
    meaning is not the same everywhere - "%08.4d" combines the '0' flag
    with a precision, and the standard says a precision makes the flag
    inert - and passed a void pointer to %s.  It now uses conversions
    whose meaning this dialect defines and a null char pointer, since
    what that case exercises is the scratch accounting, not those
    corners.

Library
~~~~~~~

  * io/heap.c: a release now merges the free neighbours in the single
    walk that finds the block, instead of walking the arena a second
    time.  The list keeps one invariant - no two adjacent blocks are
    free - which makes the merge a local operation; a release is
    therefore roughly a third faster in a loop of allocations and
    releases over a full arena, and the walk validates both neighbours
    before it writes anything.
  * io/file.c: stdoc_stream_write copies payloads into the staging
    buffer block by block and scans only the newly staged bytes for a
    newline, so a large write no longer copies the same bytes twice.
  * io/vfprintf.c: the padding chunk is filled once per call instead of
    once per character.  "%p" now honours the '+' and ' ' flags the way
    the reference C library does, printing "+0x1234" and " 0x1234" for
    a non-null pointer and holding no sign for "(nil)".
  * include/libstdoc.h: stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf are declared
    with the GNU "format(printf)" attribute where the compiler
    understands it, so a mismatched call is caught while the program is
    built.  The header documents that "%+p" is accepted by the
    functions while "-Wformat" reports it, and how to quiet that one
    diagnostic.
  * io/stdoc_io.h: stdoc_sys_read and stdoc_sys_close are documented as
    the platform layer of the input release that stdoc_stdin is waiting
    for, rather than left looking like dead code.


Build
~~~~~

  * The warning set grew from -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror to
    include -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow,
    -Wstrict-prototypes, -Wmissing-prototypes, -Wcast-qual, -Wundef,
    -Wswitch-default, -Wdouble-promotion and -Wvla.  Every file in the
    project, tests included, builds clean under both GNU C and Clang
    with it.
  * "-no-pie" is gone from the link options.  "-static" already
    produces a non-PIE executable, and passing "-no-pie" as well only
    made Clang report it as an unused argument on every link.
  * STDOC_HEAP_SIZE is validated when the project is configured: a
    value that is not a plain decimal number, or that is below the 64
    bytes the allocator can actually work with, is rejected with a
    message instead of failing later.  The value also reaches the test
    programs now, so a test is never built against a different arena
    from the library it links.
  * A new CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) builds and tests under
    GNU C and Clang on an aarch64 runner, at the default arena size and
    at the smallest one accepted, regenerates the format golden file
    from the host C library and compares it, and checks that the
    configuration guard rejects the values it documents as impossible.


Tests
~~~~~

  * Thirteen tests under CTest, up from nine.
  * test/differential.c compiles the same source once against the host C
    library and once against libstdoc and compares the two case by case
    - over two thousand combinations of flags, width and precision,
    built at run time.  Unlike a golden file, the reference is live.
  * test/stream.c covers the staging buffer, the discard and the
    relative order of stdout and stderr, compared against golden output
    in which the two streams are merged.
  * test/largewrite.c writes 200000 bytes through the system-call
    wrapper and checks the status, the length and the bytes on a file
    and through a pipe.
  * install_and_consume installs the library into a scratch prefix and
    builds and runs test/consumer against it with find_package.
  * test/heap.c and test/heap_abort.c shape their scenarios from
    stdoc_heap_capacity instead of assuming an arena of a particular
    size, so they pass from 64 bytes upwards.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * docs/testing, docs/building, docs/formatting, docs/io and docs/heap
    were brought back in line with the code: the arena minimum, the
    warning set, the link options, the count-versus-error boundary, the
    one-pass release, the compile-time format check, and the new tests.
  * docs/building records a detail of linking without a C runtime that
    is easy to meet the hard way: a static executable whose read-write
    segment has no file-backed content is placed at a file offset past
    the end of the file and dies with SIGBUS before main, and one
    initialized object prevents it.
  * docs/testing states what is still not covered, including the retry
    for a short write and any architecture or operating system other
    than the one the project is built for.


1.0.0-beta
--------------

Released 14 October 2026.

This entry records everything from the commit "major changes"
(fbc80cd), which introduced the present implementation, through the
automation commits that followed (92a8928, dependabot.yml; 79eefdc,
the CodeQL workflow), up to the state of the tree at this release,
including the dynamic memory allocator and the documentation.


Build and project structure
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * New CMake build (CMake 3.16 or newer) producing a static archive,
    with strict warnings (-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror) for every
    file written for this project and ISO C17 with extensions off for
    the whole tree, tests included.
  * Freestanding by design: the library is built without a host C
    library (-ffreestanding -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector) and the
    tests link with -nostdlib -static -no-pie, so the library's own
    entry point, printf and string helpers are the only ones a test
    program can use.
  * Platform selection at configure time: Linux on aarch64 is
    supported, anything else fails with a message that names what is
    missing instead of silently picking an incompatible
    implementation.
  * New directory layout: assembly/ (runtime), io/ (streams,
    formatting, system calls), mem/ (allocator), include/ (public
    API), etc/ (package metadata), test/ (tests), docs/
    (documentation).
  * Installation of the archive, the headers, a CMake package
    (find_package(libstdoc)) and a pkg-config file.
  * Multi-licensing restructured: LICENSE explains the choice and
    LICENSES/ carries the full texts of GPLv2+, LGPLv2.1+ and
    Apache-2.0 together with a README on how the choice works.
  * README rewritten around the new structure.


Library
~~~~~~~

  * stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf, backed by a new formatting engine
    (stdoc_vfprintf): the conversions %d %i %u %o %x %X %c %s %p and
    %%, the flags '-', '+', ' ', '#' and '0', a numeric or '*' width,
    a '.' precision (numeric or '*'), and the length modifiers hh, h,
    l, ll, z, t and j.
  * The documented failure contract: a width or precision literal
    that does not fit in an int, a field that would not fit in the
    return value and a rejected write all fail the call with -1, a
    failed call discards what it had staged, and a stream error is
    sticky.
  * Standard streams: stdoc_stdout is buffered and flushes on a
    newline and at the end of every completed call, stdoc_stderr is
    unbuffered so ordering is preserved, stdoc_stdin is reserved for a
    future input release.
  * Operating-system layer isolated in io/syscall_linux.c (the only
    place with system-call numbers), a generic system-call trampoline
    in assembly/syscall.S and the program entry point in
    assembly/start_aarch64.S - no constructors and no atexit handling,
    which is why the formatted functions flush themselves.
  * Internal stdoc_strlen, stdoc_memcpy and stdoc_memset so the
    library links with -nostdlib without taking symbols from a host
    C library.
  * Public headers: include/libstdoc.h (ISO C17, freestanding-safe)
    and include/libstdoc.hpp (extern "C" linkage for C++ consumers).


Dynamic memory
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * stdoc_malloc and stdoc_free over a static arena of 4096 bytes that
    never asks the operating system for memory, so a leak is bounded
    by the arena size and can be counted.  First-fit allocation with
    block splitting, and merging of adjacent free blocks on release,
    so the arena returns to one block when everything is freed.  The
    size is configurable with -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=<bytes>.
  * The safe variants: stdoc_malloc_safe checks "count * size" for
    overflow, zeroes what it returns and terminates the process with a
    diagnostic on failure; stdoc_free_safe rejects pointers outside
    the heap, interior pointers, double frees and corrupted headers
    the same way.  Both exit with status 134.
  * Accounting of live bytes and live blocks, which the tests assert
    to be zero after every scenario.
  * The formatting engine now borrows its digit and padding scratch
    buffer (97 bytes) from the heap for the duration of a call and
    falls back to stack buffers when the heap is exhausted, so running
    out of heap never makes the formatted functions fail.


Tests
~~~~~

  * Nine tests under CTest: golden output for index.c and format.c,
    the same golden output from C++, the failure paths of the
    formatted functions, the heap and its leak accounting, and the
    four fail-fast scenarios of the safe allocator, each of which has
    to end with the documented fatal status 134.
  * The golden file of test/format.c was produced by a reference C
    library, so the comparison checks the dialect and not the
    implementation's habits.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * New docs/ directory documenting the whole repository in plain
    text: docs/README (the index and reading order), docs/repository
    (every top-level entry), docs/building, docs/testing,
    docs/formatting, docs/io and docs/heap.  README points at it.


Continuous integration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * .github/dependabot.yml keeps the GitHub Actions dependencies
    updated (92a8928), and .github/workflows/codeql.yml runs CodeQL
    analysis (79eefdc).  Neither file takes part in the build: the
    project builds with no network access.


Version
~~~~~~~

  * The package version is 1.0.0.  CMake versions carry no suffix, so
    the beta status of this release is recorded here in NEWS rather
    than in libstdoc.pc or the CMake package version.
